# A grammar the board calls PERFECT, reading a function call as a type cast.
#
# go is 1,189 bytes, 1,189 built, 0 orphan, 0 rubble, 0 spoil, 100.0%
# standing, 0 damage. Every instrument in this repository scores it clean.
#
# `fmt.Print("x")` is a call. Joints reads it as a conversion:
#
#   joints                      tree-sitter
#   type_conversion_expression    call_expression
#     qualified_type                selector_expression
#       package_identifier            operand: identifier
#       type_identifier               field:   field_identifier
#     interpreted_string_literal    argument_list
#                                     interpreted_string_literal
#
# Same bytes, same cuts, opposite meaning: one says "call Print with x", the
# other says "convert x to the type fmt.Print".
#
# Read this specimen beside the number. `plumb` scores this file at 5 misread
# bytes out of 996 judged - the five of `Print`, which is the only LEAF whose
# name moves. Every other byte here sits under a leaf both parsers agree on,
# so a byte-indexed comparison files them plumb. The structure above the
# leaves is where this defect lives, and 9.24% is a floor partly because of
# cases exactly like this one.
roots 1
mends 0
holds call_expression
holds selector_expression
holds field_identifier
lacks type_conversion_expression
lacks qualified_type
spans field_identifier 47 52
